1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,808
Hi, I'm Derric.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:08,189
This is Gridline, my historical wind forecast
review project.

3
00:00:08,321 --> 00:00:13,939
For a UK electricity utility, purchasing
decisions depend on expected demand

4
00:00:13,939 --> 00:00:17,043
and the generation likely to be available.

5
00:00:17,175 --> 00:00:22,460
Wind adds uncertainty, because the forecast can
change before delivery.

6
00:00:22,592 --> 00:00:27,418
Gridline focuses on one part of that problem:
looking back at the wind

7
00:00:27,418 --> 00:00:32,726
forecast available at a particular decision
time, then comparing it with what

8
00:00:32,726 --> 00:00:34,932
was generated the following day.

9
00:00:35,064 --> 00:00:40,168
Let me walk through the application first, then
the implementation.

10
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:46,892
The dashboard covers purchasing decision dates
from the first to the thirty-

11
00:00:46,892 --> 00:00:49,494
first of January, twenty twenty-six.

12
00:00:49,627 --> 00:00:54,728
Each decision maps to next-day delivery, so the
delivery period runs from the

13
00:00:54,728 --> 00:00:57,908
second of January through the first of February.

14
00:00:58,042 --> 00:01:03,554
I'm using the EPEX thirty-minute auction
scenario, with a fifteen-thirty

15
00:01:03,554 --> 00:01:04,626
London cutoff.

16
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:09,969
The coverage panel shows seven hundred and
forty-four matched hourly pairs,

17
00:01:09,969 --> 00:01:14,622
and all fourteen hundred and eighty-eight half-
hour actual records.

18
00:01:14,756 --> 00:01:19,166
That tells us how much data supports the monthly
figures.

19
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:25,723
Here, I can choose a decision date and inspect
its following delivery day.

20
00:01:25,855 --> 00:01:31,387
The solid green line is the selected forecast,
and the dashed line is actual

21
00:01:31,387 --> 00:01:32,188
generation.

22
00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:37,733
I'll open the tenth of January, which maps to
delivery on the eleventh.

23
00:01:37,865 --> 00:01:40,550
The gap is visible across the day.

24
00:01:40,682 --> 00:01:46,280
The values table also exposes the publication
time behind each forecast.

25
00:01:46,412 --> 00:01:51,602
That timestamp matters: a forecast published
after the decision cutoff would

26
00:01:51,602 --> 00:01:55,153
give the analysis information the buyer didn't
have.

27
00:01:55,285 --> 00:01:59,968
Gridline chooses the latest eligible publication
for each hour.

28
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:05,168
It doesn't use later revisions to make the
historical result look better.

29
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:12,050
The monthly mean absolute error is one thousand,
eight hundred and seventy-

30
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:13,255
three megawatts.

31
00:02:13,388 --> 00:02:18,752
That's the average size of the hourly
difference, ignoring direction.

32
00:02:18,884 --> 00:02:23,993
Root mean squared error is two thousand, three
hundred and twenty-seven

33
00:02:23,993 --> 00:02:28,239
megawatts; larger misses carry more weight in
that measure.

34
00:02:28,372 --> 00:02:34,397
Mean bias is positive one thousand, seven
hundred and nine megawatts, so this

35
00:02:34,397 --> 00:02:38,700
source forecast tends to sit above the aligned
actuals.

36
00:02:38,833 --> 00:02:44,852
WAPE is sixteen point six percent: total
absolute error divided by total

37
00:02:44,852 --> 00:02:47,108
absolute actual generation.

38
00:02:47,241 --> 00:02:51,722
These figures describe differences between the
source series.

39
00:02:51,854 --> 00:02:57,167
They don't directly measure money saved or
establish a purchasing strategy.

40
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:03,564
The Daily accuracy page makes the monthly result
easier to investigate.

41
00:03:03,696 --> 00:03:08,986
Each row keeps the decision date and delivery
date together, alongside error

42
00:03:08,986 --> 00:03:10,517
measures and coverage.

43
00:03:10,648 --> 00:03:16,157
The tenth of January has a daily mean absolute
error of about four thousand,

44
00:03:16,157 --> 00:03:18,983
eight hundred and eighty-one megawatts.

45
00:03:19,115 --> 00:03:23,915
The eighteenth is much lower, at about two
hundred and fifty-two.

46
00:03:24,047 --> 00:03:28,135
Selecting a row returns to the corresponding
hourly replay.

47
00:03:28,267 --> 00:03:33,055
I can then check whether the error was sustained
or concentrated in particular

48
00:03:33,055 --> 00:03:37,169
hours, and whether missing data could have
affected the comparison.

49
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:42,505
The Method and sources page explains the
comparison.

50
00:03:42,637 --> 00:03:47,167
WINDFOR supplies hourly forecasts in average
megawatts.

51
00:03:47,299 --> 00:03:53,115
FUELHH supplies half-hour actuals, so the
application averages both equal-

52
00:03:53,115 --> 00:03:56,023
duration actual values for each hour.

53
00:03:56,156 --> 00:03:58,310
Both must exist.

54
00:03:58,442 --> 00:04:03,355
Even under the thirty-minute auction scenario,
this remains an hourly

55
00:04:03,355 --> 00:04:04,138
comparison.

56
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:09,593
There's also a scope limitation: the forecast
and actual datasets aren't

57
00:04:09,593 --> 00:04:13,364
demonstrated to cover exactly the same wind
assets.

58
00:04:13,496 --> 00:04:18,871
I keep that warning visible because complete
coverage doesn't, by itself,

59
00:04:18,871 --> 00:04:21,890
prove that the populations are identical.

60
00:04:22,022 --> 00:04:27,168
The source links and snapshot details make those
assumptions inspectable.

61
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:33,953
That's the client view: a way to move from
monthly error, to a difficult

62
00:04:33,953 --> 00:04:37,733
delivery day, to the underlying forecast
vintage.

63
00:04:37,871 --> 00:04:43,275
The value is being able to inspect the evidence
and limitations together.

64
00:04:43,413 --> 00:04:49,162
Next, I'll show how the code preserves that
historical decision boundary.

65
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:53,302
The frontend is React, built with Vite.

66
00:04:53,408 --> 00:04:58,105
In App dot J S X, changing the auction scenario
requests the same-origin

67
00:04:58,105 --> 00:04:59,279
analysis endpoint.

68
00:04:59,386 --> 00:05:04,187
FastAPI defines that endpoint in app dot P Y and
delegates the calculation to

69
00:05:04,187 --> 00:05:06,120
backend slash analysis dot P Y.

70
00:05:06,226 --> 00:05:11,596
The response contains monthly metrics, daily
summaries, hourly observations,

71
00:05:11,596 --> 00:05:13,433
coverage, and methodology.

72
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:16,502
The API caches the two scenario results.

73
00:05:16,609 --> 00:05:21,652
Locally, Vite proxies API requests to FastAPI;
the production routing is

74
00:05:21,652 --> 00:05:23,193
configured separately.

75
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:28,846
The repository includes archived Elexon JSON
under data slash raw, plus a

76
00:05:28,846 --> 00:05:32,256
manifest recording query windows and retrieval
metadata.

77
00:05:32,364 --> 00:05:37,356
This backend reads that snapshot; it doesn't
download new Elexon data on every

78
00:05:37,356 --> 00:05:37,868
request.

79
00:05:37,977 --> 00:05:41,058
Actual records are filtered to fuel type WIND.

80
00:05:41,167 --> 00:05:46,354
Both series use startTime and generation;
forecasts also use publishTime.

81
00:05:46,463 --> 00:05:50,634
Timestamps must include a timezone and are
normalized to UTC.

82
00:05:50,743 --> 00:05:53,293
Numeric values must be finite.

83
00:05:53,402 --> 00:05:57,920
Identical duplicates are collapsed, while
conflicting values for the same

84
00:05:57,920 --> 00:05:59,839
record identity raise an error.

85
00:05:59,948 --> 00:06:04,868
I didn't find a committed download
implementation, so I'm not presenting one

86
00:06:04,868 --> 00:06:05,191
here.

87
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:09,234
The main safeguard is in forecast selection.

88
00:06:09,342 --> 00:06:13,749
For each expected delivery hour, the code
collects publication times that are

89
00:06:13,749 --> 00:06:17,812
less than or equal to the decision cutoff, then
selects the latest one.

90
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:20,995
A publication exactly at cutoff is eligible.

91
00:06:21,103 --> 00:06:23,426
One second later is excluded.

92
00:06:23,534 --> 00:06:28,335
If no eligible version exists, the forecast
stays missing; a later version

93
00:06:28,335 --> 00:06:29,633
cannot fill the gap.

94
00:06:29,741 --> 00:06:34,010
The decision-date loop constructs next-day
delivery explicitly, including the

95
00:06:34,010 --> 00:06:37,170
thirty-first of January mapping to the first of
February.

96
00:06:37,278 --> 00:06:41,026
London local cutoffs are converted to UTC before
matching.

97
00:06:41,134 --> 00:06:45,181
The two scenarios change the decision boundary,
rather than changing the

98
00:06:45,181 --> 00:06:46,192
source resolution.

99
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:51,796
For each hour, actual generation is the
arithmetic mean of the observations at

100
00:06:51,796 --> 00:06:53,656
the hour and thirty minutes past.

101
00:06:53,764 --> 00:06:58,285
Those are equal-duration average-power readings,
so their mean gives hourly

102
00:06:58,285 --> 00:06:59,370
average megawatts.

103
00:06:59,479 --> 00:07:03,397
If either reading is missing, the hourly actual
is missing too.

104
00:07:03,505 --> 00:07:08,396
The metric function uses only rows with both a
forecast and an actual value.

105
00:07:08,505 --> 00:07:12,940
It calculates signed forecast-minus-actual
errors from unrounded inputs.

106
00:07:13,049 --> 00:07:16,366
Daily and monthly summaries use the same
alignment.

107
00:07:16,475 --> 00:07:21,289
WAPE returns no value when its denominator is
zero, rather than inventing a

108
00:07:21,289 --> 00:07:21,995
percentage.

109
00:07:22,104 --> 00:07:25,191
Missing observations remain visible in coverage.

110
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:31,539
The Python tests cover the month boundary,
exact-cutoff eligibility, later-

111
00:07:31,539 --> 00:07:36,403
publication exclusion, missing forecasts, actual
alignment, conflicting

112
00:07:36,403 --> 00:07:39,829
duplicates, coverage, and hand-calculated
metrics.

113
00:07:39,938 --> 00:07:42,294
They also exercise the API.

114
00:07:42,403 --> 00:07:47,269
After building the frontend, I ran the existing
suite: all sixteen tests

115
00:07:47,269 --> 00:07:47,742
passed.

116
00:07:47,851 --> 00:07:53,274
The four frontend CSV tests passed as well,
covering field mapping, missing

117
00:07:53,274 --> 00:07:56,745
values, escaping, and scenario-specific exports.

118
00:07:56,854 --> 00:07:59,734
The production frontend build also completed.

119
00:07:59,843 --> 00:08:04,455
These checks support the replay and export
behavior; they don't establish that

120
00:08:04,455 --> 00:08:09,067
the source datasets contain identical assets, or
that the application predicts

121
00:08:09,067 --> 00:08:10,191
financial outcomes.

122
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:16,056
The Dockerfile uses a Node stage to build the
frontend, then copies it into a

123
00:08:16,056 --> 00:08:19,382
Python runtime alongside FastAPI and the
archived data.

124
00:08:19,491 --> 00:08:23,812
Docker Compose exposes the combined application
on port eight thousand.

125
00:08:23,921 --> 00:08:28,015
The README documents that route and the separate
development servers.

126
00:08:28,124 --> 00:08:32,805
Vercel uses a different arrangement: the
configuration declares FastAPI and

127
00:08:32,805 --> 00:08:37,611
Vite services, routes API paths to the backend
first, then routes other paths

128
00:08:37,611 --> 00:08:38,610
to the frontend.

129
00:08:38,718 --> 00:08:41,190
The deployed dashboard is accessible.

130
00:08:41,299 --> 00:08:45,607
I haven't launched Docker or performed a
deployment during this review, so I'm

131
00:08:45,607 --> 00:08:49,915
describing the checked-in configuration, not
claiming an additional deployment

132
00:08:49,915 --> 00:08:50,191
test.

133
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:55,975
The core of Gridline is a traceable comparison:
archived source records, an

134
00:08:55,975 --> 00:09:00,734
explicit decision cutoff, next-day delivery, and
metrics computed from aligned

135
00:09:00,734 --> 00:09:01,527
observations.

136
00:09:01,632 --> 00:09:06,514
The interface makes the selected forecast,
coverage, and limitations visible

137
00:09:06,514 --> 00:09:07,092
together.

138
00:09:07,197 --> 00:09:11,445
Its boundaries are equally clear: hourly source
resolution, uncertain

139
00:09:11,445 --> 00:09:15,078
population equivalence, and prototype auction-
time cutoffs.

140
00:09:15,184 --> 00:09:19,496
Those are the points I'd validate before
extending the analysis to procurement

141
00:09:19,496 --> 00:09:20,049
decisions.

142
00:09:20,154 --> 00:09:23,750
That completes the product demonstration and
technical review.

143
00:09:23,855 --> 00:09:27,789
The screen content comes from the real
application and repository; the

144
00:09:27,789 --> 00:09:29,195
narration is AI-assisted.
